VTK's bundled loguru: a NetBSD thread id.

loguru's fallback for a thread with no name prints its id, and on platforms it
doesn't list it does static_cast<uint64_t>(pthread_self()).  NetBSD's
pthread_t is a pointer, so that doesn't compile: "static_cast from 'pthread_t'
(aka '__pthread_st *') to 'uint64_t' is not allowed" (netbsd-build-2,
2026-10-30).  Use the LWP id, as the FreeBSD (thr_self) and OpenBSD
(getthrid) branches use theirs.

--- a/ThirdParty/loguru/vtkloguru/loguru.cpp
+++ b/ThirdParty/loguru/vtkloguru/loguru.cpp
@@ -107,6 +107,8 @@
 		#include <sys/thr.h>
 	#elif defined(__OpenBSD__)
 		#include <pthread_np.h>
+	#elif defined(__NetBSD__)
+		#include <lwp.h>
 	#endif
 
 	#ifdef __linux__
@@ -991,6 +993,8 @@
 				(void)thr_self(&thread_id);
 			#elif defined(__OpenBSD__)
 				pid_t thread_id = getthrid();
+			#elif defined(__NetBSD__)
+				lwpid_t thread_id = _lwp_self();
 			#elif LOGURU_PTHREADS
 				// Here we rely on the opaque pthread_t being of integer type, which is the case on linux, but not other platforms.
 				uint64_t thread_id = static_cast<uint64_t>(pthread_self());
